      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Bronchial asthma (BA) often develops in children with atopic dermatitis (AD). Identification of factors that could predict the development of asthma in children with AD is useful for early intervention."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "We undertook a 4-year followup study to clarify the factors involved in the development of BA in infants with AD."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We registered 169 infants with AD who were free of BA at registration and examined the prevalence and characteristics of the subsequent development of BA among these patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Among the patients followed for 4 years, approximately 45% experienced asthma-like respiratory symptoms, and 35% were diagnosed as asthmatic patients by pediatric allergologists. Patients who developed BA showed early appearance of house dust mite (HDM)-specific immunoglobulin E (IgE) and persistently high levels of food-specific IgE. Male sex, a positive family history of BA, and the appearance of HDM-specific IgE were identified as significant risk factors for the early development of BA, but the significance of these parameters decreased thereafter. A positive family history of AD, the outcome of skin lesions, and keeping furred pets were also identified as risk factors in a part of the followup period. Among the parameters examined, the early appearance of HDM-specific IgE was the most significant risk factor."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Appearance of HDM-specific IgE antibodies in early childhood, which seems to be mainly influenced by genetic factors, is a major risk factor for the subsequent development of BA in children with AD, but the influence decreases after longer followup."
        }
      ]},
